      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "There are few studies which have analyzed patients' presenting symptoms to evaluate whether a particular symptom is clinically significant. This study is aimed at determining whether any particular presenting symptom is important in the preoperative evaluation of a pelvic mass."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "130 charts of patients presenting with a pelvic mass were reviewed. A standard data collection technique was used to determine rate of occurrence as well as clinical statistical relevance of specific symptoms."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "25 malignancies, 16 of gynecologic origin, were found. Of the variables studied, women older than 50 showed a 14 fold increase in RR (OR 23.3. 95 percent CI 5-104.8) and those with abdominal distention showed an OR 7.9 (95 percent CI 2-21.5)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Abdominal distention showed statistical significance and may be useful in the preoperative assessment of a pelvic mass."
        }
      ]},
